   689	# Quality AI Harness
   690
   691	Use for static guards, CI gates, regression triage, flaky test diagnosis, and durable prevention.
   692
   693	## Guard Constitution
   694
   695	A guard is not a script that makes a project look governed. It is an executable constraint for a stable failure class that has happened, can recur, and can be recognized without guessing.
   696
   697	A guard is worth keeping only when it satisfies these conditions:
   698
   699	1. It names the failure class clearly.
   700	2. It proves value with a red fixture, green fixture, historical bad case, synthetic broken case, or other negative proof.
   701	3. It scopes itself narrowly enough to avoid noisy false positives.
   702	4. Its failure output tells the implementer where the problem is, why it failed, and what repair path or Skill to use.
   703	5. It belongs to a declared layer: Core, app profile, paper profile, or one project namespace.
   704	6. It can run non-interactively with stable exit codes.
   705	7. It has a cost level and does not block fast loops with slow runtime or screenshot work.
   706	8. It has a retirement path when the rule becomes obsolete, too narrow, or replaced.
   707
   708	Do not promote a guard just because a bug was fixed once. If the rule only protects the current file, current screenshot, current wording, or current pixel coordinate, keep it as task evidence or a local test unless it can be generalized.
   709
   710	## Three Gate Layers
   711
   712	Use three layers instead of forcing every lesson into `scripts/quality`.
   713
   714	| Layer | Location | Use when |
   715	|---|---|---|
   716	| Documentation / process gate | `AGENTS.md`, `develop/Harness/*`, `docs/*`, `skills/*` | The issue is a workflow rule, ownership rule, routing rule, or repair procedure. |
   717	| Static gate | `scripts/quality/*` | The failure can be found from source, config, generated artifacts, contracts, or manifests. |
   718	| Runtime / QA gate | `scripts/qa/*` | The failure needs execution, screenshots, device/runtime evidence, integration, rendering, or build artifacts. |
   719
   720	Every substantial fix should decide whether each layer is needed. If a layer is not needed, record why.
   721
   722	## Stage and Severity
   723
   724	Stage and severity are separate.
   725
   726	Stage:
   727
   728	- `candidate`: a rule extracted from a problem, not yet trusted.
   729	- `shadow`: implemented as report-only or non-blocking, collecting false positives and misses.
   730	- `enforced`: trusted enough to block its configured loop.
   731	- `deprecated`: kept for history or transition, not a default gate.
   732
   733	Severity:
   734
   735	- `advisory`: informational; never blocks.
   736	- `warning`: visible but non-blocking by default.
   737	- `blocking`: blocks the configured local or CI gate.
   738	- `release-gate`: blocks release, main-branch merge, or publication package readiness.
   739
   740	Do not make all new guards blocking. New rules usually start as `candidate` or `shadow` unless they protect a proven high-risk contract.
   741
   742	## Acceptance Bar
   743
   744	Blocking and release gates need evidence before promotion:
   745
   746	- Red proof: historical bad case, fixture, mutation, synthetic broken example, or replayable failing artifact.
   747	- Green proof: recommended implementation, legal boundary case, and allowed exception if exceptions exist.
   748	- Scope proof: include/exclude rules or trigger conditions that avoid unrelated files.
   749	- Repair proof: failure output points to a Skill, docs, component, token, adapter key, or command.
   750
   751	For visual, runtime, and paper-figure guards, a red/green proof may be a minimal render fixture, intentionally broken generated asset, screenshot manifest, or geometry/metadata sample rather than source code.
   752
   753	## Evidence Contract
   754
   755	All guards must print a human-readable summary and return meaningful exit codes.
   756
   757	Blocking and release gates should also support an evidence directory when practical:
   758
   759	```txt
   760	agents-results/YYYY-MM-DD/<task>/quality-gates/<guard-name>.md
   761	agents-results/YYYY-MM-DD/<task>/quality-gates/<guard-name>.json
   762	```
   763
   764	Markdown is for humans. JSON is for CI, dashboards, `harnessctl`, and AI repair loops.
   765
   766	## Budget
   767
   768	Guard cost belongs to the profile or project adapter.
   769
   770	- Fast static checks can run in pre-commit or local quick checks.
   771	- Medium checks fit pre-push or local CI.
   772	- Slow, external, runtime, screenshot, device, or publication-build checks belong in QA or release gates.
   773
   774	Do not put expensive screenshot, simulator, Appium, LaTeX, or network-dependent work into fast static loops unless the project explicitly opts in.
   775
   776	## Waiver and Retirement
   777
   778	Waivers are allowed only when local, justified, and reviewable. Avoid broad folder-level disables.
   779
   780	A guard should be deprecated or removed when:
   781
   782	- the design system, framework, schema, or workflow changed;
   783	- it is replaced by a better guard;
   784	- it is too narrow to justify its cost;
   785	- false positives stay higher than its prevention value;
   786	- the failure class no longer exists.
   787
   788	Retirement must update the guard card, related Skills/docs, and registry or adapter entries.
